On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 22:26:45 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> - Dovecot alone writes the mailbox. OpenSMTPD hands list mail to
>>   Dovecot over LMTP, and mbsync syncs Fastmail with Dovecot over IMAP.
>
> Explain this more, I don't understand it.

Under the hub, three programs change the list on lab-mail:

- OpenSMTPD adds every message someone posts.
- mbsync adds mail that arrives at Fastmail, and copies your moves and
  flag changes both ways.
- Dovecot moves or deletes a message when your read-write login does.

The question is whether all three write the maildir files themselves,
or whether two of them ask Dovecot to do it.

In the second way, Dovecot is the only program that opens the files.
OpenSMTPD does not write the maildir. It hands each message to Dovecot
over LMTP, a cut-down SMTP made for the last hop into a mailbox, and
Dovecot files it. [inference, from my reading of smtpd.conf(5)] That
is one "action ... lmtp" line in place of "action ... maildir". mbsync
does not write the maildir either. Today its two ends are Fastmail over
IMAP and a local maildir. They would become Fastmail over IMAP and
Dovecot over IMAP on 127.0.0.1, with a login of its own. Your moves
already go through Dovecot, since you reach lab-mail over IMAP.

So every change to the list goes through one server, as one user, and
Dovecot's ACL decides who may do what: your login and mbsync's may
write, and agent logins may only read.

>>   [inference] Today mbsync writes the maildir and Dovecot only reads
>>   it. With three writers, the chmod after each pull and the separate
>>   owners stop working.
>
> Explain.

Today the files keep agents read-only, not just Dovecot's config.
mbsync runs as its own user and creates each file 0600. After each
pull, the fetch job adds read for group and other, so Dovecot's user
can read every message and write none. Even a mistake in Dovecot's
ACL could not let an agent delete list mail, because Dovecot itself
cannot. The spec chose this on purpose, and rejected running Dovecot
as mbsync's user for that reason.

That works because only one user ever writes. Under the hub, Dovecot
must move and delete files for you, mbsync must rename files when a
flag or folder changes on Fastmail, and OpenSMTPD must create files.
If each runs as its own user and writes directly:

- [fact] Moving or deleting a message in a maildir is a rename or an
  unlink, which needs write on the folder. Every writer then needs
  write on every folder, so a shared group with 0770 folders and 0660
  files. mbsync creates 0700 and 0600 whatever the umask, so the job
  would have to chmod after every pull, now to add write, and any
  file in the gap before that chmod is one Dovecot cannot move.
- [fact] Dovecot's user can then write the files. Read-only for
  agents rests on Dovecot's ACL alone, which is what the spec tried
  to avoid.
- [inference] Each IMAP server or sync tool keeps its own record of
  the files: Dovecot its dovecot-uidlist, mbsync its sync state and
  the ",U=" in each file name. Both cope with a file that appears from
  outside. Two of them renaming the same files at the same time, each
  with its own UID scheme, is where maildir sharing is known to go
  wrong, as duplicated or lost flags and messages fetched twice.

With Dovecot as the only writer, the chmod goes away and so do the
separate owners. Dovecot's user owns the maildir, and nothing races
it. The one cost is the one in the first point. Agents being
read-only rests on Dovecot's ACL, not on file permissions. Whichever
way we pick, that is true once your login can delete. I think one
writer is the better of the two, and the spec's alternatives section
would say why.

>>   ~/mail/<user>_at_p7x.dev for both. Agents send with git send-email
>
> We can just call this <user>@p7x.dev, I know that's a valid dir
> name. Nothing wrong with that, right?

Nothing wrong with it. [fact] A FreeBSD file name may hold any byte
except "/" and NUL, and "@" is not special to sh(1), mbsync, or
notmuch. It only means something to scp or rsync, in a
"user@host:path" argument, and that needs a colon that a local path
never has.
I picked _at_ from your earlier "~/mail/pat_at_p7x.dev" in "Re: How
to configure beastie to use lab-mail" [1]. The spec will use
~/mail/<user>@p7x.dev.

> I will import the maildir the first time.
>
> Yes, retire the old mechanism.

Understood. The spec will name the import as your one step before the
switch. Its end state will have no mail bookmark, no submissions
remote, and no ~/lab/mail.jj for mail.

>> - Point 2: archive to an IMAP folder (a) or a beastie-only tag (b)?
>
> Archive by dir, not tag.
>
> Also we will probably know what Fastmail calls the dir once the first
> sync takes place. It has an Archive dir already.

Good. [inference] Fastmail marks that folder with the \Archive
special-use flag over IMAP, so the spec can refer to it as Fastmail's
archive folder and leave the exact name to the first sync.

